# Acrylic & Synthetic Polymers Curriculum Cache
#
# Free-radical PMMA synthesis, emulsion coalescence, WWII cockpit canopies, and the Pop Art revolution.

#>spw_cache_mat_acrylic
#:cache #!acrylic_and_synthetic_polymers
#:layer #!pragmatics

^"intent"{
  ~#goal: "Model the free-radical polymerization of methyl methacrylate, amorphous PMMA optical physics, waterborne emulsion coalescence, WWII aviation glazing, and the mid-century acrylic art revolution."
  ~#protocol: "cache.layer/1"
}

^"polymer_chemistry_and_optical_physics"{
  "free_radical_polymerization_and_pmma_structure": .{
    chain_growth_addition_mechanism: "Methyl methacrylate monomer (CH2=C(CH3)COOCH3) initiated by azo (AIBN) or peroxide (BPO) free radicals; radical addition across vinyl double bonds yielding high molecular weight Poly(methyl methacrylate) PMMA with un-hydrolyzable all-carbon covalent backbone",
    thermal_and_mechanical_properties: "Amorphous atactic thermoplastic with glass transition temperature T_g ~ 105 C; rigid and shatter-resistant at room temperature; thermoformable above 125 C into complex compound curves",
    optical_transmission_superiority: "Total visible light transmittance of 92% (surpassing standard soda-lime glass ~88%); refractive index n = 1.49; Abbe dispersion number V_d = 58; exceptional UV resistance and outdoor weathering stability without yellowing",
  }[reg=facet],
  "waterborne_acrylic_emulsion_coalescence": .{
    colloidal_latex_architecture: "Sub-micron polymer beads (50-200nm) suspended in water via anionic surfactants; Minimum Film Forming Temperature (MFFT) governing polymer chain mobility",
    three_stage_film_coalescence: "Stage 1: Water evaporation concentrating latex spheres into close-packed arrays; Stage 2: Capillary force overcoming electrostatic repulsion to deform spheres into polyhedra; Stage 3: Autohesion and interdiffusion of polymer chains across particle boundaries, annealing into a seamless, flexible, water-insoluble plastic film within 30 minutes",
  }[reg=facet],
}

^"biomedical_and_heavy_engineering_applications"{
  "aviation_glazing_and_extreme_aquariums": .{
    shatterproof_transparent_armor: "Plexiglas, Lucite, Perspex replacing brittle laminated glass; submarine pressure hulls and deep-sea exploration spheres; massive structural aquarium viewing panels (up to 700mm thickness) carrying hydrostatic loads exceeding thousands of metric tons",
  }[reg=facet],
  "orthopedic_bone_cement_and_intraocular_lenses": .{
    charnley_orthopedic_bone_cement: "Sir John Charnley: self-curing PMMA powder/liquid cold-curing cement anchoring total hip and knee arthroplasty prostheses into trabecular bone, dampening cyclic impact stresses",
    ridley_intraocular_lenses_iol: "Sir Harold Ridley observing WWII Spitfire pilots surviving cockpit canopy punctures with PMMA splinters in their eyes without ocular inflammation; inventing the first biocompatible intraocular replacement lenses to cure cataract blindness globally",
  }[reg=facet],
}

^"world_history_and_the_mid_century_artistic_explosion"{
  "otto_rohm_to_wwii_aviation_bubbles": .{
    otto_rohm_commercialization_1933: "Otto Rohm's pioneering 1901 doctoral research at University of Tubingen culminating in the 1933 commercial synthesis of Plexiglas; instant adoption by aircraft manufacturers",
    allied_and_axis_canopy_engineering: "Every major WWII combat aircraft—Supermarine Spitfire, Messerschmitt Bf 109, B-17 Flying Fortress, Lancaster bomber—fitted with blown PMMA bubble canopies, gun turrets, and nose cones, providing 360-degree aerodynamic visibility without deadly glass fragmentation casualties",
  }[reg=facet],
  "liquitex_and_the_pop_art_revolution": .{
    golden_and_bocour_synthetic_mediums: "Leonard Bocour and Sam Golden developing Magna (1947) and Liquitex (1955) as the first commercially available artists' acrylic emulsion paints; water-cleanup, non-toxic, drying in 20 minutes with zero yellowing or surface cracking",
    liberation_of_color_field_and_pop_art: "Helen Frankenthaler's soak-stain raw unprimed canvas technique; Morris Louis, Kenneth Noland; Andy Warhol utilizing fast-drying acrylics for high-speed photographic silkscreens of Campbell's Soup and Marilyn Monroe; David Hockney's crisp, glare-filled flat Los Angeles swimming pools",
  }[reg=facet],
}

^"wonder_probes"{
  #>wonder_mat_acrylic_1
  ?["How did the accidental observation of acrylic aircraft canopy shrapnel inside the eyes of blinded WWII pilots lead to the cure for human cataract blindness?"]{
    #:depth #!biomaterials ~#lens(serendipitous_biocompatibility_discovery)
    ~#hypothesis: "British ophthalmologist Sir Harold Ridley operated on RAF fighter pilots whose Perspex cockpit canopies were shattered by bullet strikes. Unlike glass splinters (which triggered violent foreign-body immune inflammation and eye enucleation), PMMA canopy shards remained inert inside the ocular anterior chamber for months without tissue rejection. Ridley recognized PMMA's bio-inert surface chemistry and cast the first artificial intraocular lens (IOL), restoring vision to tens of millions of cataract patients."
    !probe{ "Evaluate macrophage activation and foreign-body giant cell encapsulation metrics on PMMA surfaces vs soda-lime silicate glass in anterior chamber animal models." }
    $%[biomaterials.pmma_ocular_immune_tolerance_score, clinical.cataract_iol_implant_success_rate]
  }
}
